# Daedalus configuration
#
# Copy to .env (./daedalus.sh setup does this for you) and edit as needed.
# .env is gitignored; this file is the tracked template.
#
# ./daedalus.sh, sync.sh and reset.sh read it, and so does docker compose for
# the one optional container (SearXNG).

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ports
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# `./daedalus.sh start`: the dashboard and API on one port.
DAEDALUS_PORT=8000

# SearXNG, when it is running. Localhost only — the instance exists for one
# process on this machine.
#
# 8081 rather than SearXNG's usual 8080: Odysseus publishes its own instance on
# 8080, and the two projects are developed on the same machine.
SEARXNG_PORT=8081

# `./daedalus.sh dev`: the API and the vite dev server (hot reload).
BACKEND_PORT=8000
FRONTEND_PORT=5173

# Metrics and container logs — Prometheus/Grafana on their own port. Optional
# and not built yet (TODO.md, M7): when this is empty the Databases panel says
# the stack is not configured rather than linking into nothing.
DAEDALUS_OBSERVABILITY_URL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The five databases
#
# They are separate on purpose. The sensor database is opened read-only by the
# driver itself, so a bug in ingestion or logging physically cannot reach the
# telemetry of record. See docs/PROJECT.md §6.3.
#
# They all live in the repo, and the scripts set their paths themselves (see
# scripts/common.sh:host_py) so every entry point resolves the same files:
#     data/sqlite/sensor_readings.db   sensor telemetry
#     logs/ai_logs.db                  audit logs
#     data/chroma/                     vector store (embedded)
#     data/sqlite/chat.db              chat transcripts
#     backend/data/prefs.db            UI preferences
#
# So there is nothing to configure below for the default layout. Anything you
# run against the backend by hand should go through `host_py` for the same
# reason, or paths.py falls back to backend/data/.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 1. SENSOR TELEMETRY — SQLite, READ-ONLY to the AI layer.
#    Written by the SCADA ingestion subsystem; opened read-only regardless.
#    Resolves to data/sqlite/sensor_readings.db.

# 2. AUDIT & EVALUATION LOGS — SQLite, read/write.
#    Chat, tool-call, retrieval, model, error, feedback and memory logs.
#    Resolves to logs/ai_logs.db.

# 3. KNOWLEDGE VECTOR STORE — ChromaDB, read/write, embedded in the API
#    process and writing to data/chroma. Nothing to set.

# 3b. WEB SEARCH — SearXNG, read-only, and a *setup* surface (Rule 1/Rule 5).
#     Not part of the answer path: web results are for finding and checking the
#     documents M2 ingests, never for answering a question. The runtime stays
#     offline, so it runs only when asked for. It is the one thing that still
#     needs Docker (docker-compose.yml); without Docker, web search reports
#     itself unavailable and nothing else changes:
#
#         ./daedalus.sh dev --with-search
#
#     This is only the default address. A URL saved in Settings → Search wins,
#     and the other five providers (Brave, Tavily, …) are configured there with
#     their keys, which are stored in prefs.db rather than here.
SEARXNG_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081

# Off by default, and think before turning it on. Set this to
# /var/run/docker.sock and Settings → Search can start and stop the SearXNG
# container from the UI instead of you running a command. Your user must be in
# the `docker` group for the API to open it.
#
# What you are granting: a process that can reach the Docker socket can do
# anything Docker can on this machine — including to containers belonging to
# other projects. Daedalus itself only ever touches `daedalus-searxng`, but that
# limit binds Daedalus' code, not everything else in the API process. The
# agent's `bash` and `python` tools run as the same user, so if you enable
# this, lock `execute_code` in Settings → Agent Tools.
# DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock

# Optional. Blank means the container generates one on first boot and keeps it
# in the searxng-data volume, which is what you want unless you are restoring
# an instance and need its cookies to stay valid.
# SEARXNG_SECRET=

# 4. CHAT TRANSCRIPTS — SQLite, read/write.
#    Conversation sessions and messages: the assistant's memory within and
#    across sessions. Separate from the audit log on purpose — a user owns and
#    may delete their transcript, while audit rows are append-only evidence.
#    Resolves to data/sqlite/chat.db.

# 5. UI PREFERENCES — SQLite, read/write.
#    Theme and interface state. Deliberately server-side: nothing is kept in
#    browser storage.
#    Resolves to backend/data/prefs.db.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local model runtime
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Ollama installed on this machine (https://ollama.com/download). Point this
# elsewhere to use an Ollama on another host.
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hardware detection (the Forge)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Detection runs on a schedule in the backend instead of on every panel open —
# it costs seconds per pass (a subprocess for the GPU, an HTTP timeout for
# Ollama, and under WSL a ~2.5s PowerShell interop call), so paying it per open
# made the cost scale with how often anyone looked.
#
# Three tiers. Unchanging facts (CPU model, cores, platform, the Windows host's
# totals) are probed once at boot and never again. The rest refresh on these:
#
#   LIVE  — memory, CPU load, free disk. Pure syscalls, under 2ms for all three.
#   SLOW  — Ollama. An HTTP call with a timeout (2.1s when the daemon is down),
#           and reachability only changes when somebody starts or stops it.
#
# The GPU picks its own tier from what answered: with nvidia-ml-py installed it
# is a 13ms in-process call and refreshes with the live numbers; without it,
# nvidia-smi costs ~600ms (a Windows interop call under WSL) and it drops to
# the slow tier. Nothing to configure — it measures and decides.
#
# Nothing refreshes at all unless the panel is open — the loop goes dormant
# IDLE_AFTER seconds after the last read, so a closed panel costs nothing.
# Worth raising the live interval on a laptop with hybrid graphics, or lowering
# it while watching memory during a benchmark.
#
# Seconds. Defaults shown.
DAEDALUS_HW_LIVE_INTERVAL=30
DAEDALUS_HW_SLOW_INTERVAL=300
DAEDALUS_HW_IDLE_AFTER=120

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SearXNG container limits
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Hard caps for the one optional container. Defaults are in
# docker-compose.yml; uncomment to override.
#
# SEARXNG_MEM_LIMIT=512m
# SEARXNG_CPUS=1.0

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Notes
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There are no secrets here, and that is deliberate: Daedalus runs fully local
# with no cloud APIs in the runtime. If that ever changes, put credentials in
# .env (never .env.example) and keep .env gitignored.
